  "mechanismSummary": "Boc-alanine and DCC first give the O-acylisourea, as in DCC amide coupling. HOBt gives its proton to the isourea nitrogen, and OBt⁻ adds to the acyl carbon; the intermediate expels neutral dicyclohexylurea, leaving an OBt active ester. Glycine methyl ester then adds to the ester and OBt⁻ leaves, giving the protected dipeptide Boc-Ala-Gly-OMe and HOBt again. HOBt matters because the O-acylisourea is too reactive: on its own it rearranges to an unreactive N-acylurea, or cyclises to an oxazolone whose α-hydrogen is acidic, so the amino acid racemises. The OBt ester is reactive enough to meet the amine but too mild for these side reactions. The app does not check stereochemistry yet.",
  "references": [
    "Clayden, Greeves and Warren, Organic Chemistry, 2nd edition (Oxford University Press), chapter 23",
    "Montalbetti and Falque, Amide bond formation and peptide coupling, Tetrahedron 2005, 61, 10827"
  ],
